diagnosis_text:
The data-center fabric is currently healthy, and there is no evidence of a failed leaf–spine link. End-to-end reachability from pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) to pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2) is normal: topology-wide tests show 0% loss with ~1 ms RTT in both directions. The BGP control-plane across the CLOS is fully established. On both leaves, show ip bgp summary reports two established spine neighbors with prefixes exchanged (e.g., leaf_router_0_0 shows 172.16.0.4 and 172.16.0.8 in Established with PfxRcd=1). Both spines have all three neighbors (super-spine plus two leaves) up and exchanging routes (PfxRcd=1–2 as expected), and super_spine_router_0 has both spine neighbors up. At Layer 1/2, ip -br link on all routers shows eth0/eth1/eth2 are UP, LOWER_UP; there are no downed interfaces on the alleged leaf–spine links.

Mechanistically, if a fabric link had failed, we would expect a down peer, reduced ECMP path count, or at minimum a BGP session flap; none are observed. Localization checks on every router and relevant interfaces show everything operational.

Root cause: No current network fault; the reported outage is not reproducible and likely transient or host-side at the time of the report.

Corrective fix: No restart is required. Keep the fabric as-is. If the issue recurs, capture timestamps and check interface counters and BGP logs for flaps, and verify host NIC/ARP at the endpoints.
=== END ===